# Step 7g, 2026-09-22 — `PROGRESS.md` row 37's finding (23), and the row this file's own header
# predicted without noticing it was already owed: "a file whose bytes a test pins is protected from
# the slice that lands it". `config::adr0083_config_type_declared_in_kernel_and_threaded_into_run_
# inputs` recomputes the committed run record's `run_id` — `sha256_lp([SCHEMA, stage,
# canonical(inputs)])` — and `replay::read_run` reads every input file with NO newline
# normalisation, so every `\r` under this tree is inside the hash preimage.
#
# 🛑 THE TREE IS MIXED, AND THAT IS WHY NEITHER EXPORT DIRECTION WAS FAITHFUL. Measured 2026-09-22:
# SIX files in the preimage were smudged (worktree CRLF, index blob LF) — the five
# `inputs/catalog/*.toml` AND `inputs/pack/incident-deviation.toml`, which finding (23) did not name
# — while `inputs/facets.toml` (13,031 B), `inputs/artifacts/artifacts.toml` and the four `.json`
# inputs were LF in both. So an `autocrlf=true` clone smudged `facets.toml` too and moved the digest;
# an `autocrlf=false` clone un-smudged the six and moved it the other way. 22 of 143 kernel fixture
# files were CRLF in the working tree, so a fresh clone rewrote the other 121 — the figure Checkpoint
# 7g's own guard (7) comment records.
#
# ⚠️ THE ROW PINS THE BYTES AS THEY ARE; IT DOES NOT NORMALISE THEM TO LF, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
# The recorded `run_id` was blessed over these exact mixed bytes, and **the run record's DIRECTORY
# NAME IS THAT DIGEST** (`RECORDED_RUN`). Normalising would move the digest and force a rename of the
# directory — which is named in **accepted ADR-0130**, in two CLOSED stages' prose (`s7b`, `s7c`),
# in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, in `crates/rk-kernel/src/replay.rs` and in two dated spike records.
# ADR-0092 decision 1: an accepted record is never edited. Pinning the bytes makes every checkout on
# every platform reproduce the preimage the digest already commits to, which is the property that was
# missing, and it costs no record a correction.
crates/rk-kernel/tests/fixtures/logs/** -text

